00:27:03,840 --> 00:27:07,340
90 cent, 0.9 euros per dollar.

511
00:27:08,120 --> 00:27:11,760
Well, suppose I expect 1 euro for dollar in the next period.

512
00:27:12,380 --> 00:27:13,660
What will happen to exchange today?

513
00:27:13,860 --> 00:27:15,060
Well, it jumps today to 1.

514
00:27:16,300 --> 00:27:17,080
Why is that?

515
00:27:29,360 --> 00:27:31,080
That's your friend, the trader there.

516
00:27:35,600 --> 00:27:39,750
But yes, that's true.

517
00:27:42,780 --> 00:27:43,120
That's true.

518
00:27:43,600 --> 00:27:47,110
What does that mean, though?

519
00:27:47,770 --> 00:27:51,970
It is true, it's more expensive, but why did you want to buy it to start with?

520
00:27:52,590 --> 00:27:56,330
I mean, who cares that something is more expensive if you are not trying to buy it?

521
00:28:00,010 --> 00:28:04,070
Because the current price of something also has to take into account its future price.

522
00:28:04,950 --> 00:28:06,530
That's what the question says, yes?

523
00:28:09,650 --> 00:28:14,510
Because it's valued at the present moment, takes into account so much value in the future.

524
00:28:14,830 --> 00:28:18,590
You know, this is an arbitrage-type relationship.

525
00:28:18,590 --> 00:28:22,250
And what I suggest is whenever you come across an arbitrage

526
00:28:22,250 --> 00:28:25,870
type argument, you ask the question, well, suppose not.

527
00:28:27,130 --> 00:28:28,510
Suppose this didn't happen.

528
00:28:29,250 --> 00:28:30,230
What would then happen?

529
00:28:30,450 --> 00:28:31,450
What would look odd?

530
00:28:33,330 --> 00:28:36,110
Almost any arbitrage, that's a good way of thinking about it.

531
00:28:36,150 --> 00:28:38,390
It says, OK, the question tells me that the exchange

532
00:28:38,390 --> 00:28:39,350
has to jump right away.

533
00:28:40,770 --> 00:28:41,510
Well, suppose not.

534
00:28:41,830 --> 00:28:42,530
What goes wrong?

535
00:28:44,870 --> 00:28:47,950
I think that's the easiest way to think

536
00:28:47,950 --> 00:28:50,450
about any of these asset pricing in general, by the way.

537
00:28:52,110 --> 00:28:52,870
Well, suppose not.

538
00:28:53,270 --> 00:28:54,970
Suppose that the spectral exchanger goes up,

539
00:28:55,130 --> 00:28:56,190
the interest rates haven't changed,

540
00:28:56,810 --> 00:28:58,310
and the exchange rate today doesn't move.

541
00:28:58,870 --> 00:28:59,650
What happens then?

542
00:29:00,970 --> 00:29:02,690
Remember, we've started a situation with both.

543
00:29:03,670 --> 00:29:04,570
Interest rates are the same.

544
00:29:05,410 --> 00:29:08,950
Now the spectral exchanger went up by 10%, say.

545
00:29:12,030 --> 00:29:14,250
And the current exchange rate hasn't moved.

546
00:29:15,910 --> 00:29:18,310
I'm sure between the two of you can design this trade.

547
00:29:19,510 --> 00:29:20,250
What do you do?

548
00:29:25,930 --> 00:29:28,170
Everyone would buy four bonds in the next period,

549
00:29:29,070 --> 00:29:30,750
no one in the first period.

550
00:29:33,930 --> 00:29:35,490
No, no, but what do you do today?

551
00:29:39,400 --> 00:29:41,860
Suppose you're a trader, and then now you see,

552
00:29:42,020 --> 00:29:44,700
whoops, the dollar will appreciate 10%,

553
00:29:44,700 --> 00:29:45,880
the interest rates are the same,

554
00:29:46,140 --> 00:29:47,560
and the exchange rate is not moving today.

555
00:29:47,700 --> 00:29:48,080
What do you do?

556
00:29:58,800 --> 00:29:59,820
Which bond do you buy?

557
00:30:04,160 --> 00:30:04,700
I love it.

558
00:30:05,200 --> 00:30:08,100
Of course, because you have a 10% expected capital gain

559
00:30:08,100 --> 00:30:10,260
from buying that bond, if that doesn't happen.

560
00:30:10,720 --> 00:30:12,620
The two bonds are paying the same interest rate.

561
00:30:12,740 --> 00:30:14,900
And now I tell you, well, yeah, but one is going to appreciate

562
00:30:14,900 --> 00:30:17,160
by 10% relative to the other.

563
00:30:18,300 --> 00:30:21,300
So clearly, you go short massively the foreign bond,

564
00:30:21,380 --> 00:30:22,920
and you go very long the US bond.

565
00:30:23,240 --> 00:30:23,820
That's what you do.

566
00:30:25,280 --> 00:30:26,380
We all want to do the same.

567
00:30:26,480 --> 00:30:27,440
So it happens very quickly.

568
00:30:28,680 --> 00:30:30,460
And the exchanges appreciate today

569
00:30:31,060 --> 00:30:34,480
up to a point in which that incentive is no longer there.

570
00:30:35,580 --> 00:30:38,080
And in this particular case, if the interests are the same,

571
00:30:38,140 --> 00:30:39,880
that will happen only if the exchange rate

572
00:30:39,880 --> 00:30:43,940
jumps exactly by the same amount as the expected value

573
00:30:45,420 --> 00:30:48,540
of the dollar change in the future.

574
00:30:51,820 --> 00:30:52,440
Think about this.

575
00:30:52,480 --> 00:30:53,020
Play with these things.

576
00:30:53,120 --> 00:30:54,000
I know it can be confusing.

577
00:30:54,920 --> 00:30:58,120
And I always start with, let me move something.

578
00:30:59,120 --> 00:31:01,220
The equation tells me this has to happen

579
00:31:01,220 --> 00:31:04,580
to exchange it, well, suppose that didn't happen to exchange it.

580
00:31:05,140 --> 00:31:08,020
And then you say, oh, then I clearly invest in this bond.

581
00:31:08,160 --> 00:31:09,060
This dominates the other one.

582
00:31:09,520 --> 00:31:11,320
Well, that condition tells you, no, no.

583
00:31:11,740 --> 00:31:13,560
In equilibrium, you have to be indifferent.

584
00:31:14,300 --> 00:31:16,320
So the only thing that can move is exchange rate.

585
00:31:16,920 --> 00:31:19,460
And exchange rate has to move until you're indifferent again

586
00:31:20,020 --> 00:31:21,880
after you have done some argument

587
00:31:22,460 --> 00:31:23,600
on the right-hand side.

588
00:31:24,720 --> 00:31:26,280
That's the way you need to think about it.

589
00:31:27,660 --> 00:31:34,880
So here, I'm just plotting this relationship

590
00:31:35,660 --> 00:31:38,240
in the space of exchange rate in the x-axis

591
00:31:38,240 --> 00:31:41,140
and the domestic interest rate here.

592
00:31:42,380 --> 00:31:44,420
So that's an upward-sloping relation.

593
00:31:44,700 --> 00:31:48,080
You can see here, as I move the interest rate up or the other

594
00:31:48,080 --> 00:31:48,500
way around.

595
00:31:48,660 --> 00:31:50,600
But anyways, if I move the interest rate up,

596
00:31:51,240 --> 00:31:52,220
the exchange rate is going up.

597
00:31:52,380 --> 00:31:53,740
So that's a positive relationship.

598
00:31:54,720 --> 00:31:55,800
I can do it the other way around.

599
00:31:55,800 --> 00:31:57,080
as they move the exchange rate up,

600
00:31:57,180 --> 00:31:58,760
then the domestic interest rate has to go up.

601
00:31:59,400 --> 00:32:03,080
I'm taking as parameters the foreign interest rate

602
00:32:03,780 --> 00:32:05,720
and the expected exchange rate.

603
00:32:05,860 --> 00:32:07,480
I take as parameter this and that,

604
00:32:07,940 --> 00:32:09,220
then I have a positive relationship

605
00:32:09,220 --> 00:32:12,460
between the exchange rate and the domestic interest rate.

606
00:32:13,280 --> 00:32:14,820
So that's going to be the ui.

607
00:32:14,860 --> 00:32:17,660
I'm plotting the ui p and covering interest parity

608
00:32:17,660 --> 00:32:18,100
condition.

609
00:32:19,340 --> 00:32:20,900
Notice this point here is interesting.

610
00:32:21,780 --> 00:32:25,200
This point tells you that when the domestic interest rate,

611
00:32:25,200 --> 00:32:28,140
if i is equal to international interest rate,

612
00:32:28,900 --> 00:32:32,380
then the exchange rate has to be equal to the expected

613
00:32:32,380 --> 00:32:34,580
exchange rate, which is the question I asked before.

614
00:32:35,640 --> 00:32:36,480
Remember, I asked you a question.

615
00:32:36,600 --> 00:32:39,460
Well, suppose that we start with an interest rate that

616
00:32:39,460 --> 00:32:44,160
is equal to the international interest rate, what

617
00:32:44,820 --> 00:32:45,660
is the exchange rate?

618
00:32:45,920 --> 00:32:47,840
And you said the answer was, well,

619
00:32:47,860 --> 00:32:49,600
it has to be equal to the expected exchange rate.

620
00:32:50,000 --> 00:32:50,900
That's that point here.

621
00:32:52,850 --> 00:32:57,110
If the domestic interest rate is above that,

622
00:32:57,550 --> 00:33:01,650
the international interest rate, then the exchange rate today

623
00:33:01,650 --> 00:33:03,830
has to be above the expected exchange rate,

624
00:33:04,230 --> 00:33:06,430
because that will give you a expected depreciation

625
00:33:06,430 --> 00:33:08,570
of the currency, which will compensate for the fact

626
00:33:08,570 --> 00:33:11,890
that the domestic bond is paying a higher interest

627
00:33:11,890 --> 00:33:13,110
rate than international bond.

628
00:33:15,050 --> 00:33:18,950
Conversely, if the domestic bond is paying a lower interest

629
00:33:18,950 --> 00:33:21,470
rate, then the exchange rate today is very depreciated,

630
00:33:21,470 --> 00:33:24,150
because you have to expect it to appreciate in order

631
00:33:24,150 --> 00:33:26,830
to compensate for the interest rate differential.

632
00:33:29,190 --> 00:33:34,290
Probably not, but this requires practice, I tell you.

633
00:33:40,550 --> 00:33:45,650
OK, but now we have an equation for the exchanger, at least.

634
00:33:46,470 --> 00:33:50,750
So I can go back to my IS, is the IS equation

635
00:33:50,750 --> 00:33:53,490
in the open economy, and I will have an equation

636
00:33:53,490 --> 00:33:55,150
for the exchange, so I can replace it.

637
00:33:55,970 --> 00:34:00,230
This is nice, because I have two new parameters, expected

638
00:34:00,230 --> 00:34:02,410
exchange rate and international interest rate,

639
00:34:02,710 --> 00:34:04,470
but now this is also a function of the interest rate.

640
00:34:04,590 --> 00:34:07,550
So at this moment, I have one equation and two unknowns,

641
00:34:07,550 --> 00:34:09,490
really, after I solve out for the exchanges.

642
00:34:10,030 --> 00:34:11,690
I have one equation and two unknowns.

643
00:34:11,690 --> 00:34:14,650
The two unknowns are its output and the domestic interest rate.

644
00:34:15,790 --> 00:34:16,810
All the rest are parameters.

645
00:34:17,650 --> 00:34:20,610
So that's the same situation we're at in lecture three

646
00:34:20,610 --> 00:34:21,030
or so.

647
00:34:21,030 --> 00:34:23,250
So then we need an extra equation.

648
00:34:24,470 --> 00:34:27,470
The extra equation was monetary policy, the LM.

649
00:34:28,470 --> 00:34:29,950
We're going to do exactly the same here.

650
00:34:31,230 --> 00:34:31,910
The LM is the same.

651
00:34:32,110 --> 00:34:35,030
It's a domestic central bank, sets the interest rate.

652
00:34:35,510 --> 00:34:36,070
So now I'm set.

653
00:34:37,650 --> 00:34:40,670
Now we have the ISLM model in the open economy.

654
00:34:41,290 --> 00:34:42,510
This is the Mandel Fleming model.

655
00:34:43,230 --> 00:34:44,770
That's what the Mandel Fleming model is.

656
00:34:45,990 --> 00:34:57,300
So yes, a more complicated I.S. with a U.I.P. driven exchange

657
00:34:57,700 --> 00:35:01,300
rate, and then the L.M. is the same as in the closed economy.

658
00:35:05,390 --> 00:35:07,090
So this is the Mandel Fleming law.

659
00:35:11,640 --> 00:35:14,380
So one thing we know already, we knew from the previous

660
00:35:14,380 --> 00:35:18,040
lecture, that we have a smaller multiply in the open

661
00:35:18,040 --> 00:35:19,940
economy, because we have the imports that are also

662
00:35:19,940 --> 00:35:20,720
responding to out.

663
00:35:20,960 --> 00:35:21,860
We have a new parameter.

664
00:35:21,860 --> 00:35:29,760
But now we also know that an increase in the interest rate, so monetary policy in the open economy, has two effects now.

665
00:35:30,160 --> 00:35:33,240
It used to have only this effect. Remember, it affected domestic investment.

666
00:35:33,440 --> 00:35:41,160
So an increase in the interest rate would lead to a reduction in aggregate demand because investment would fall.

667
00:35:42,280 --> 00:35:43,880
Remember, that was the role of the interest rate.

668
00:35:44,540 --> 00:35:48,840
That's the way monetary policy worked in the closed economy, was through this channel here.

669
00:35:50,240 --> 00:35:52,460
Now, we have a second channel, which is this one.

670
00:35:54,930 --> 00:35:56,230
So when the interest rate goes up,

671
00:35:57,090 --> 00:35:58,450
it's contractionary for two reasons.

672
00:35:58,670 --> 00:36:00,550
One, for the reason we had before,

673
00:36:00,710 --> 00:36:02,070
which is that investment falls.

674
00:36:02,630 --> 00:36:04,690
But there is a second reason, it's contractionary.

675
00:36:06,930 --> 00:36:07,850
What is that second reason?

676
00:36:18,920 --> 00:36:19,900
I mean, it's only here.

677
00:36:20,200 --> 00:36:20,760
It's the only second.

678
00:36:21,520 --> 00:36:21,540
Yeah?

679
00:36:21,660 --> 00:36:22,640
To raise the exchange rate.

680
00:36:23,260 --> 00:36:24,740
Because it appreciates the exchange rate.

681
00:36:24,900 --> 00:36:25,960
And when you appreciate the exchange rate,

682
00:36:26,080 --> 00:36:27,020
net exports decline.

683
00:36:27,940 --> 00:36:29,300
So more of the domestic consumption

684
00:36:29,300 --> 00:36:30,680
is diverted to foreign goods.

685
00:36:31,340 --> 00:36:36,720
unless a foreign demand is allocated to our exports, okay?

686
00:36:37,180 --> 00:36:38,480
So that's the second channel.

687
00:36:38,740 --> 00:36:40,540
So in an open economy, and the smallest economy,

688
00:36:40,980 --> 00:36:43,800
the more important is this term, the more powerful is that channel.

689
00:36:53,140 --> 00:36:55,160
The US cares very little about this effect.

690
00:36:56,740 --> 00:36:59,220
Most other economies care a lot about this effect, okay?

691
00:37:01,580 --> 00:37:04,300
Because the US is a relatively close economy, believe it or not.

692
00:37:05,440 --> 00:37:09,480
So this is sort of the star diagram of the Mandel-Flaming model.

693
00:37:10,920 --> 00:37:15,140
So this thing here is our old ISLM model.

694
00:37:15,740 --> 00:37:17,800
It's just that this IS is a little thicker now.

695
00:37:17,940 --> 00:37:19,720
It has net exports in there and so on,

696
00:37:19,720 --> 00:37:21,020
but it looks exactly the same.

697
00:37:21,580 --> 00:37:26,720
That is, plots equilibrium in financial and goods market,

698
00:37:27,520 --> 00:37:30,340
the combinations of output and domestic interest rate

699
00:37:30,340 --> 00:37:34,200
that are consistent with equilibrium in both markets.

700
00:37:34,440 --> 00:37:35,400
That's the case here.

701
00:37:36,040 --> 00:37:37,960
This is the IS, which is all the combinations

702
00:37:37,960 --> 00:37:41,220
of domestic output and domestic interest rate

703
00:37:41,220 --> 00:37:43,360
that are consistent with equilibrium in goods market.

704
00:37:44,220 --> 00:37:45,760
This is the interest rate that is consistent

705
00:37:45,760 --> 00:37:47,120
with equilibrium in financial markets.

706
00:37:47,260 --> 00:37:48,800
That's what the Fed does in the US.

707
00:37:49,900 --> 00:37:53,500
That point is where both markets are in equilibrium.

708
00:37:54,480 --> 00:37:55,860
But we can take this interest rate.

709
00:37:55,980 --> 00:37:56,640
So that's what will happen.

710
00:37:56,840 --> 00:37:58,460
The interest rate will be in the US.

711
00:37:58,620 --> 00:38:00,560
The interest rate is set by the Fed, not by the ECB.

712
00:38:00,880 --> 00:38:02,300
The Fed will set the interest rate.

713
00:38:03,000 --> 00:38:04,780
That will give us some equilibrium output.

714
00:38:06,100 --> 00:38:09,660
And then we can go to the UIP condition you see I'm plotting

715
00:38:09,660 --> 00:38:11,860
here and figure out what the exchange rate is.

716
00:38:12,960 --> 00:38:16,160
Because for this interest rate here, there's going to be some

717
00:38:16,160 --> 00:38:19,140
point in the UIP, and that tells me exactly what the

718
00:38:19,140 --> 00:38:19,860
exchange rate is.

719
00:38:22,110 --> 00:38:25,990
So with this set of diagrams, I can determine the interest

720
00:38:25,990 --> 00:38:27,270
rate, output, and exchange rate.

721
00:38:31,100 --> 00:38:33,840
So I can study the effects of different policies, for

722
00:38:33,840 --> 00:38:36,720
example, on output, the interest rate, of course.

723
00:38:37,460 --> 00:38:39,820
that's the policy itself, and exchange rate.

724
00:38:40,040 --> 00:38:41,960
So this is the new thing I can explain.

725
00:38:42,100 --> 00:38:43,600
I can do a little bit of asset pricing here.

726
00:38:43,860 --> 00:38:48,300
I can explain the behavior of the exchange rate as well.

727
00:38:52,140 --> 00:38:53,660
So this diagram, I mean, you need

728
00:38:53,660 --> 00:38:55,420
to really control very, very well.

729
00:38:56,000 --> 00:38:58,420
So I'm going to play with it quite a bit.

730
00:39:00,240 --> 00:39:02,020
Monetary policy, let's do monetary policy.

731
00:39:02,080 --> 00:39:03,500
We talk about monetary policy already.

732
00:39:04,320 --> 00:39:05,960
So suppose that for whatever reason,

733
00:39:05,960 --> 00:39:13,180
Then the domestic economy, the domestic central bank decides to hike interest rate.

734
00:39:13,440 --> 00:39:17,460
Suppose the economy was overheating, output was too high, relative to natural rate of

735
00:39:17,460 --> 00:39:20,660
output for the typical reasons why you need to raise interest rate.

736
00:39:21,400 --> 00:39:23,580
And so suppose that the domestic interest rate goes up.

737
00:39:24,420 --> 00:39:27,160
Well, as it used to be, that's going to be contractionary.

738
00:39:28,420 --> 00:39:29,540
What happens to exchange rate?

739
00:39:31,180 --> 00:39:33,980
Well, I know the interest rate went up.

740
00:39:35,040 --> 00:39:39,540
I go, I look into my UIP for the high interest rate,

741
00:39:39,620 --> 00:39:41,360
and in a current exchange rate, it's above,

742
00:39:42,860 --> 00:39:44,940
they all, it has to go up, relatively tall.

743
00:39:45,260 --> 00:39:47,720
When I increase interest rate from here to there,

744
00:39:48,220 --> 00:39:49,900
then my exchange rate has to appreciate.

745
00:39:50,760 --> 00:39:51,420
Why is that?

746
00:39:53,060 --> 00:39:56,760
So an expansionary domestic monetary policy will lead

747
00:39:56,760 --> 00:40:00,200
to a contraction in output, which is what we get

748
00:40:00,200 --> 00:40:04,820
out of monetary policy, but it will also

749
00:40:04,820 --> 00:40:06,920
lead to an appreciation of the currency.

750
00:40:07,760 --> 00:40:08,080
Why is that?

751
00:40:13,240 --> 00:40:14,360
That's what we just discussed.

752
00:40:14,880 --> 00:40:15,140
It's UAP.

753
00:40:16,000 --> 00:40:18,220
If I move the domestic interest rate,

754
00:40:18,760 --> 00:40:21,040
and the rest of the world does not follow me,

755
00:40:21,520 --> 00:40:22,820
so we move interest rate dead on,

756
00:40:23,740 --> 00:40:25,920
then now I need to compensate for this increase

757
00:40:25,920 --> 00:40:27,060
in the interest rate differential.

758
00:40:27,360 --> 00:40:30,380
And the compensation will come through an expected capital

759
00:40:30,380 --> 00:40:32,180
loss through the currency.

760
00:40:32,180 --> 00:40:36,200
So if I appreciate more of the currencies and I haven't moved the expected exchange rate,

761
00:40:36,620 --> 00:40:41,180
I expect a larger loss from the currency side.

762
00:40:43,200 --> 00:40:45,100
That's what has happened here.

763
00:40:45,160 --> 00:40:46,220
That's what is behind the precision.

764
00:40:47,460 --> 00:40:56,340
And of course, the precision is already built in here, which is what makes monetary policy

765
00:40:56,340 --> 00:41:00,560
more powerful than the close economy, because you get the next export channel.

766
00:41:00,560 --> 00:41:01,960
